(function(){
  window.AP_LEARNING=window.AP_LEARNING||{};
  const M=(id,title,highYield,teaching,keyPoints,visualTitle,visualItems,mnemonic,goodToKnow,practiceConnection,prompt,options)=>({id,title,highYield,teaching,keyPoints,visual:{title:visualTitle,kind:'concept-map',items:visualItems.map(x=>({label:x[0],detail:x[1]}))},mnemonic:{title:mnemonic[0],cue:mnemonic[1],explanation:mnemonic[2]},goodToKnow,practiceConnection,activity:{prompt,options:options.map(x=>({text:x[0],correct:x[1],feedback:x[2]}))}});
  window.AP_LEARNING['lesson-12']={version:1,title:'The Endocrine System',chapter:12,duration:'90–110 minutes',
    outcomes:['Distinguish endocrine from exocrine signaling and compare major hormone classes.','Explain receptor specificity and negative-feedback control.','Trace hypothalamus–pituitary target-gland axes.','Connect thyroid, parathyroid, adrenal and pancreatic hormones with their core variables.','Organize reproductive and pineal hormones by source and function.','Recognize textbook disorder patterns as vocabulary rather than diagnostic tools.'],
    modules:[
      {id:'hormone-logic',title:'How hormones communicate',summary:'Hormones travel widely but act only where the right receptor and cellular machinery are present.',moments:[
        M('l12-m1','Endocrine versus exocrine','Ductless endocrine glands release to blood; exocrine glands use ducts.',[
          'Endocrine glands secrete hormones into interstitial fluid and then the bloodstream. Hormones circulate throughout the body, but only target cells with suitable receptors respond. Exocrine glands send products through ducts to a surface or lumen.',
          'Neural and endocrine control interact: neural signals are typically rapid and targeted, whereas hormonal signals may begin more slowly and persist longer. The hypothalamus is a central bridge between the systems.'
        ],['Endocrine glands are ductless.','Hormones enter circulation.','Receptors create target specificity.','Exocrine glands use ducts.','Hypothalamus links nervous and endocrine control.'],'Signal comparison',[['Endocrine','Blood-borne hormone'],['Target cell','Matching receptor'],['Exocrine','Duct to surface/lumen'],['Neural','Rapid directed signal']],['ENDO enters blood','Endocrine goes inward to circulation; exocrine exits by duct','Use route, not product name alone.'],'The pancreas has both endocrine islets and exocrine digestive tissue.',
        'Nutrition interacts with endocrine variables, but hormone symptoms require clinical assessment and laboratory context.',
        'Which statement describes an endocrine gland?',[['It releases hormone into circulation without a duct.',true,'Correct. Endocrine glands are ductless.'],['It always releases through a duct.',false,'That describes exocrine secretion.'],['It acts on every cell equally.',false,'Only receptor-bearing target cells respond.']]),
        M('l12-m2','Hormone chemistry and receptors','Protein, steroid and modified amino-acid hormones signal differently.',[
          'Protein and peptide hormones, including insulin and growth hormone, are water soluble and generally bind membrane receptors, activating internal second-messenger pathways. Steroid hormones, including cortisol and testosterone, are lipid soluble and bind intracellular receptors that influence gene expression.',
          'Modified amino-acid hormones include epinephrine and thyroid hormones. Their behavior depends on structure: catecholamines use membrane receptors, while thyroid hormone acts more like a lipid-soluble signal.'
        ],['Insulin and GH are protein hormones.','Cortisol and testosterone are steroids.','Epinephrine is a modified amino acid.','Water-soluble hormones use membrane receptors.','Steroids use intracellular receptors.'],'Hormone classes',[['Protein/peptide','Insulin, GH'],['Steroid','Cortisol, testosterone'],['Modified amino acid','Epinephrine, thyroid hormone']],['PSA','Protein · Steroid · Amino-acid derivative','Class first, then attach examples.'],'A hormone’s chemistry helps predict receptor location, not whether it is “stronger.”',
        'Supplements marketed as “hormone balancing” should be evaluated critically; foods do not act as interchangeable replacements for prescribed hormones.',
        'Which pair contains steroid hormones?',[['Cortisol and testosterone',true,'Correct. Both derive from cholesterol.'],['Insulin and growth hormone',false,'Those are protein hormones.'],['Epinephrine and insulin',false,'They belong to different classes.']])
      ]},
      {id:'feedback-pituitary',title:'Feedback and the pituitary hub',summary:'Hormone axes are regulated loops, not one-way chains.',moments:[
        M('l12-m3','Negative feedback','The response reduces the original drive and stabilizes a variable.',[
          'In negative feedback, rising hormone or target-variable levels inhibit further secretion from upstream sources. For example, hypothalamic TRH stimulates pituitary TSH, which stimulates thyroid hormone; adequate thyroid hormone then reduces TRH and TSH drive.',
          'Positive feedback amplifies a process for a limited event. Oxytocin-driven uterine contractions during childbirth are a classic example: contraction promotes additional oxytocin signaling until birth ends the loop.'
        ],['Negative feedback opposes change.','Rising end hormone can inhibit upstream signals.','TRH → TSH → thyroid hormone.','Oxytocin can participate in positive feedback.'],'Feedback loop',[['Hypothalamus','Releasing hormone'],['Anterior pituitary','Tropic hormone'],['Target gland','End hormone'],['Feedback','End hormone reduces upstream drive']],['TRH tells TSH','TRH from hypothalamus triggers pituitary TSH','Then thyroid hormone feeds back.'],'Feedback is dynamic; a single hormone measurement is interpreted with timing and related signals.',
        'Dietary intake can influence substrates such as iodine, but it does not replace evaluating the entire regulatory axis.',
        'What happens in endocrine negative feedback?',[['A rising output inhibits continued secretion upstream.',true,'Correct. The loop resists further change.'],['Every hormone rises without limit.',false,'That would not stabilize the system.'],['A duct carries hormone to the target.',false,'Endocrine hormones circulate without ducts.']]),
        M('l12-m4','Anterior and posterior pituitary','One lobe synthesizes several hormones; the other stores and releases hypothalamic hormones.',[
          'The pituitary, or hypophysis, has two major lobes. The anterior pituitary produces growth hormone, TSH, ACTH, FSH, LH, prolactin and MSH. GH supports growth and metabolism; MSH can increase pigmentation; LH helps trigger ovulation; prolactin supports milk production after birth.',
          'The posterior pituitary stores and releases oxytocin and antidiuretic hormone made in the hypothalamus. Oxytocin supports uterine contractions and milk ejection; ADH promotes water conservation by the kidneys.'
        ],['Pituitary is also called hypophysis.','Anterior lobe makes GH, TSH, ACTH, FSH, LH, prolactin, MSH.','LH triggers ovulation.','Prolactin supports milk production.','Posterior lobe releases ADH and oxytocin.'],'Pituitary split',[['Anterior','Makes multiple tropic/direct hormones'],['Posterior','Stores/releases hypothalamic ADH + oxytocin'],['LH','Ovulation'],['Prolactin','Milk production'],['MSH','Pigmentation']],['FLAT PiG + M','FSH · LH · ACTH · TSH · Prolactin · GH · MSH','A common checklist for anterior-pituitary hormones.'],'Milk production (prolactin) and milk ejection (oxytocin) are different processes.',
        'Lactation nutrition support should account for physiology and personal goals without interpreting endocrine disorders.',
        'Which anterior-pituitary hormone triggers ovulation?',[['Luteinizing hormone',true,'Correct. The LH surge triggers ovulation.'],['Oxytocin',false,'Oxytocin is released from the posterior pituitary.'],['Aldosterone',false,'Aldosterone is an adrenal-cortex hormone.']])
      ]},
      {id:'thyroid-calcium',title:'Metabolic rate and calcium balance',summary:'The thyroid and parathyroids regulate different variables despite their close location.',moments:[
        M('l12-m5','Thyroid hormones and iodine','Thyroid hormone supports metabolic activity; calcitonin participates in calcium regulation.',[
          'TSH stimulates the thyroid to produce T3 and T4, hormones that increase metabolic activity in many tissues. Iodine is required to build these hormones. Inadequate iodine can contribute to thyroid enlargement, called goiter, though goiter has more than one possible cause.',
          'Excess thyroid-hormone production is associated with hyperthyroid states such as Graves disease. Severe adult hypothyroid patterns were historically described with myxedema, including fluid accumulation in subcutaneous tissues; diagnosis depends on clinical and laboratory evaluation.'
        ],['TSH stimulates thyroid.','T3/T4 require iodine.','Goiter means enlarged thyroid.','Graves disease is associated with excess thyroid activity.','Myxedema is a severe hypothyroid pattern.'],'Thyroid chain',[['TRH','Hypothalamus'],['TSH','Anterior pituitary'],['T3/T4','Thyroid'],['Iodine','Hormone substrate'],['Feedback','T3/T4 reduce upstream drive']],['T3/T4 need I','Thyroid hormone requires iodine','Adequacy matters; more is not automatically better.'],'Excess iodine can also disrupt thyroid function; avoid treating an assumed deficiency without assessment.',
        'A nutrition professional can assess iodine sources and adequacy while medical care evaluates thyroid function.',
        'What dietary element is required to synthesize T3 and T4?',[['Iodine',true,'Correct. Iodine is built into thyroid hormones.'],['Iron only',false,'Iron has many roles but is not the defining thyroid-hormone element.'],['Calcium',false,'Calcium is regulated by other endocrine pathways.']]),
        M('l12-m6','Parathyroid hormone and calcitonin','Low blood calcium activates PTH; calcitonin generally opposes that direction.',[
          'When blood calcium falls, parathyroid hormone (PTH) raises it by acting on bone and kidneys and by supporting vitamin-D activation, which increases intestinal calcium absorption. The net effect is to defend extracellular calcium needed for nerve and muscle function.',
          'Calcitonin from thyroid C cells tends to lower blood calcium by discouraging bone resorption, although its role in adult moment-to-moment regulation is smaller than PTH’s. Excess PTH can demineralize and deform bone over time.'
        ],['Parathyroids release PTH.','PTH responds to low blood calcium.','PTH supports renal retention and vitamin-D activation.','Calcitonin tends to lower blood calcium.','Chronic excess PTH can weaken bone.'],'Calcium directions',[['Low Ca²⁺','PTH rises'],['Kidney','Retain Ca²⁺/activate vitamin D'],['Intestine','Absorb more with active vitamin D'],['Bone','PTH can increase release'],['Calcitonin','Opposing direction']],['PTH Pulls calcium To Higher blood levels','PTH raises extracellular calcium','Attach the direction before the mechanisms.'],'Blood calcium is tightly controlled, so a normal blood value does not by itself prove optimal skeletal calcium stores.',
        'Assess calcium and vitamin-D intake without promising that diet alone corrects a parathyroid disorder.',
        'Which gland responds when blood calcium is too low?',[['Parathyroid',true,'Correct. It releases PTH.'],['Pineal',false,'The pineal releases melatonin.'],['Pituitary only',false,'It is not the direct calcium-sensing gland in this pathway.']])
      ]},
      {id:'adrenal-stress',title:'Adrenal layers and stress responses',summary:'Cortex and medulla have different origins, hormones and time courses.',moments:[
        M('l12-m7','Adrenal cortex zones','Mineral balance, glucose/stress and androgens map from outer to inner cortex.',[
          'The adrenal cortex produces steroid hormones. Its outer zona glomerulosa makes mineralocorticoids such as aldosterone, which promote sodium retention and potassium excretion. The middle zona fasciculata makes glucocorticoids such as cortisol, which influence metabolism and stress adaptation.',
          'The inner zona reticularis produces adrenal androgens. Too little adrenal-cortex output is associated with Addison disease; chronic excess, especially cortisol, with Cushing syndrome. These are clinical diagnoses, not conclusions from body shape or fatigue alone.'
        ],['Cortex makes steroids.','Outer cortex makes mineralocorticoids.','Cortisol is a glucocorticoid.','Inner cortex makes androgens.','Cushing = excess cortex hormones; Addison = deficiency.'],'Cortex layers',[['Glomerulosa','Salt/mineralocorticoids'],['Fasciculata','Sugar/glucocorticoids'],['Reticularis','Sex/androgens']],['GFR: Salt, Sugar, Sex','Glomerulosa · Fasciculata · Reticularis','Move outer to inner with the three S words.'],'Cortisol has normal essential functions; “cortisol is bad” is biologically inaccurate.',
        'Sleep, meal regularity and stress-management habits can support wellbeing but are not treatment substitutes for adrenal disease.',
        'What does the outer adrenal cortex chiefly secrete?',[['Mineralocorticoids',true,'Correct. Aldosterone is the key example.'],['Melatonin',false,'That comes from the pineal.'],['Insulin',false,'That comes from pancreatic beta cells.']]),
        M('l12-m8','Adrenal medulla','Epinephrine and norepinephrine rapidly support fight-or-flight physiology.',[
          'The adrenal medulla is functionally linked to the sympathetic nervous system. Its chromaffin cells release epinephrine and norepinephrine into blood, extending a rapid challenge response throughout the body.',
          'These catecholamines can increase cardiac activity, redirect blood flow and help mobilize fuel. They are modified amino-acid hormones, unlike the steroid hormones produced by the cortex.'
        ],['Medulla releases epinephrine and norepinephrine.','These are fight-or-flight hormones.','They are modified amino-acid derivatives.','Medulla is linked to sympathetic output.','Cortex hormones are steroids.'],'One gland, two systems',[['Cortex','Slower steroid signals'],['Medulla','Rapid catecholamines'],['Epinephrine/NE','Fight-or-flight']],['Medulla = emergency middle','The inner medulla releases rapid challenge signals','Contrast it with the steroid cortex.'],'A short stress response is adaptive; chronic symptoms have many possible contributors.',
        'Nutrition counselling can address practical eating patterns under stress without claiming to normalize catecholamines.',
        'Which pair is released by the adrenal medulla?',[['Epinephrine and norepinephrine',true,'Correct. They are catecholamine fight-or-flight hormones.'],['Insulin and glucagon',false,'Those are pancreatic hormones.'],['T3 and T4',false,'Those are thyroid hormones.']])
      ]},
      {id:'fuel-reproduction-rhythm',title:'Fuel, reproduction and biological time',summary:'Pancreas, gonads and pineal gland regulate distinct but interacting variables.',moments:[
        M('l12-m9','Insulin and glucagon','Pancreatic islets defend blood glucose in opposite directions.',[
          'Beta cells release insulin when blood glucose rises. Insulin supports glucose uptake in responsive tissues and storage, so blood glucose falls. Alpha cells release glucagon when glucose is low; glucagon stimulates the liver to break down glycogen and produce/release glucose.',
          'Diabetes mellitus involves inadequate insulin production, impaired insulin action, or both. Polydipsia means excessive thirst; polyuria is excessive urination; polyphagia is excessive hunger. These signs require assessment rather than self-diagnosis.'
        ],['Beta cells make insulin.','Alpha cells make glucagon.','Insulin lowers circulating glucose.','Glucagon raises it through liver action.','Polydipsia means excessive thirst.'],'Glucose seesaw',[['High glucose','Insulin from beta cells'],['Storage/uptake','Glucose falls'],['Low glucose','Glucagon from alpha cells'],['Liver output','Glucose rises']],['I lowers; G gives','Insulin lowers blood glucose; glucagon gives glucose back','Use the directional contrast.'],'Insulin and glucagon often act together across meals and fasting rather than as isolated switches.',
        'Nutrition care is central in diabetes management but should align with diagnosis, medications and individualized plans.',
        'Which pancreatic cells secrete insulin?',[['Beta cells',true,'Correct. Beta cells are the insulin source.'],['Alpha cells',false,'Alpha cells secrete glucagon.'],['Oxyphil cells',false,'Those are found in parathyroid tissue.']]),
        M('l12-m10','Gonads, prolactin and melatonin','Reproductive signals and circadian timing complete the endocrine map.',[
          'The ovaries produce estrogens and progesterone; testes produce testosterone. FSH and LH from the anterior pituitary regulate gonadal function, with LH triggering ovulation. Testosterone and related anabolic steroids have androgenic and anabolic effects; nonmedical use carries risks.',
          'Prolactin supports milk production after childbirth. The pineal gland secretes melatonin, which helps regulate circadian rhythms. Seasonal changes in light can influence melatonin timing, but seasonal affective disorder is more complex than simply “too much melatonin.”'
        ],['FSH/LH regulate gonads.','LH triggers ovulation.','Prolactin supports milk production.','Pineal gland makes melatonin.','Melatonin helps set circadian rhythms.'],'Reproduction and rhythm',[['Gonads','Sex steroids'],['FSH/LH','Pituitary control'],['Prolactin','Milk production'],['Pineal','Melatonin'],['Melatonin','Circadian timing']],['PLM','Prolactin · LH · Melatonin','Milk production, ovulation, biological night.'],'Anabolic steroids are variants related to testosterone; they are not the same category as stimulants or depressants.',
        'Sleep timing can affect eating patterns; address habits and environment without treating sleep or mood disorders outside scope.',
        'Which hormone helps regulate circadian rhythms?',[['Melatonin',true,'Correct. It is secreted by the pineal gland.'],['Aldosterone',false,'That regulates sodium and potassium handling.'],['Prolactin',false,'That supports milk production.']])
      ]}
    ],
    caseStudy:{title:'After a meal: a coordinated endocrine story',scenario:'A learner says one high-carbohydrate meal permanently “breaks hormones” and that every tired feeling must be high cortisol.',prompts:['Trace insulin and glucagon around a meal and between meals.','Explain negative feedback using one pituitary axis.','Contrast adrenal cortex and medulla signals.','Identify why symptoms alone cannot establish an endocrine diagnosis.','Offer scope-safe nutrition education without fear-based claims.'],takeaway:'Endocrine regulation is dynamic, receptor-specific and feedback controlled; nutrition matters within a larger physiologic and clinical context.'},
    recap:['Endocrine glands are ductless; target cells respond through specific receptors.','Protein, steroid and modified amino-acid hormones use different signaling routes.','Negative feedback stabilizes endocrine axes.','Anterior pituitary makes several hormones; posterior pituitary releases ADH and oxytocin.','Thyroid hormone requires iodine; PTH defends blood calcium.','Adrenal cortex makes steroids; medulla makes catecholamines.','Insulin lowers and glucagon raises blood glucose.','Melatonin helps regulate circadian rhythm.'],
    flashcards:[{front:'Endocrine gland',back:'Ductless gland that releases hormone into circulation.',tag:'principles'},{front:'Protein hormone',back:'Water-soluble hormone such as insulin or growth hormone.',tag:'class'},{front:'Steroid hormone',back:'Cholesterol-derived hormone such as cortisol or testosterone.',tag:'class'},{front:'Negative feedback',back:'Rising output reduces continued upstream secretion.',tag:'control'},{front:'Hypophysis',back:'Another name for the pituitary gland.',tag:'pituitary'},{front:'Luteinizing hormone',back:'Anterior-pituitary hormone whose surge triggers ovulation.',tag:'pituitary'},{front:'Prolactin',back:'Hormone supporting milk production.',tag:'pituitary'},{front:'Goiter',back:'Enlargement of the thyroid gland.',tag:'thyroid'},{front:'Parathyroid hormone',back:'Hormone that raises blood calcium when it falls.',tag:'calcium'},{front:'Mineralocorticoid',back:'Adrenal-cortex hormone class regulating electrolytes; aldosterone is the example.',tag:'adrenal'},{front:'Beta cell',back:'Pancreatic islet cell that releases insulin.',tag:'pancreas'},{front:'Glucagon',back:'Pancreatic hormone that raises blood glucose through liver action.',tag:'pancreas'},{front:'Polydipsia',back:'Excessive thirst.',tag:'vocabulary'},{front:'Melatonin',back:'Pineal hormone involved in circadian timing.',tag:'pineal'}],
    practice:[{prompt:'Contrast endocrine and exocrine glands.',answer:'Endocrine glands release hormones into circulation without ducts; exocrine glands release through ducts.',rationale:'Route of secretion defines the categories.',tag:'principles'},{prompt:'Classify insulin, cortisol and epinephrine.',answer:'Protein, steroid and modified amino-acid hormones.',rationale:'Chemistry predicts receptor and signaling patterns.',tag:'class'},{prompt:'Trace the thyroid axis.',answer:'Hypothalamic TRH → pituitary TSH → thyroid T3/T4 → negative feedback.',rationale:'This is a classic three-level endocrine axis.',tag:'control'},{prompt:'Which pituitary lobe makes GH, TSH and LH?',answer:'Anterior pituitary.',rationale:'The posterior lobe releases hypothalamic ADH and oxytocin.',tag:'pituitary'},{prompt:'Why can iodine deficiency contribute to goiter?',answer:'Low thyroid-hormone synthesis can increase upstream stimulation and enlarge the gland.',rationale:'The feedback loop explains the structural response.',tag:'thyroid'},{prompt:'What happens to PTH when blood calcium falls?',answer:'PTH secretion rises.',rationale:'PTH acts to restore extracellular calcium.',tag:'calcium'},{prompt:'Contrast adrenal cortex and medulla.',answer:'Cortex makes steroid hormones; medulla releases epinephrine and norepinephrine.',rationale:'They differ in chemistry, control and timing.',tag:'adrenal'},{prompt:'Which cortical zone makes mineralocorticoids?',answer:'Zona glomerulosa, the outer layer.',rationale:'Its major example is aldosterone.',tag:'adrenal'},{prompt:'Contrast insulin and glucagon.',answer:'Insulin lowers blood glucose; glucagon raises it through liver action.',rationale:'They defend glucose in opposite directions.',tag:'pancreas'},{prompt:'What hormone regulates circadian timing and where is it made?',answer:'Melatonin from the pineal gland.',rationale:'Light–dark information shapes its release.',tag:'pineal'}]
  };
})();
